// Dagric OS — which updates install themselves, and which wait to be asked.
//
// THIS FILE IS A CONFFILE OF dagric-security-policy, and that is the point of
// it living here rather than being written by 0300-hardening.hook.chroot as it
// used to be. A hook-written file is owned by no package, so the one file that
// decides whether a Dagric machine installs its own security updates could
// never be corrected on a machine that was already sold — on the channel whose
// entire purpose is correcting things. Now a fix to this policy ships like any
// other fix.
//
// 0300-hardening.hook.chroot still ASSERTS it at build time (present, parsed by
// apt, and carrying the Dagric origin), because a conffile that silently failed
// to arrive would void the product's central promise just as quietly as the
// os-release trap described below.
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot "false";
Unattended-Upgrade::Remove-Unused-Dependencies "true";

// CRITICAL — do not remove. Stock 50unattended-upgrades matches origins with
// ${distro_codename}, which unattended-upgrade resolves via lsb_release, i.e.
// from VERSION_CODENAME in os-release. Hook 0100 rebrands that to "foundation"
// (and ID to "dagric"), so EVERY stock pattern misses and nothing is ever
// upgraded — silently voiding the product's central "silent security updates"
// promise. Origins-Pattern is an apt LIST option, so these entries are
// APPENDED to the stock ones; the real Debian codename is hardcoded on
// purpose. Bump these when rebasing onto a newer Debian release.
Unattended-Upgrade::Origins-Pattern {
    "origin=Debian,codename=trixie,label=Debian";
    "origin=Debian,codename=trixie,label=Debian-Security";
    "origin=Debian,codename=trixie-security,label=Debian-Security";
    "origin=Debian,codename=trixie-updates,label=Debian";
    // Dagric's OWN security baseline, and only that package — see the
    // Package-Blacklist below, which is what keeps this line from turning
    // the whole Dagric channel into an automatic one.
    "origin=Dagric,codename=trixie,label=Dagric OS";
};

// THE DAGRIC ORIGIN IS AUTOMATIC FOR ONE PACKAGE AND CLICK-TO-INSTALL FOR THE
// REST, WHICH IS WHY THIS LIST EXISTS.
//
// Origins-Pattern is the only granularity unattended-upgrades offers, and it
// matches a whole archive. But the four Dagric packages are not alike:
//
//   dagric-security-policy   the kernel hardening sysctls and the APT policy.
//                            Its own description says it is "versioned so the
//                            baseline can evolve via updates" — and until now
//                            nothing in the Dagric origin was ever installed
//                            automatically, so a hardening regression fixed
//                            through the channel sat in Discover until the
//                            owner happened to look. Silence is defensible for
//                            exactly this class of update, and only this one.
//
//   dagric-tools             the wizard, the Hub, the manual, the helpers.
//   dagric-branding          wallpapers, the login theme, the splash.
//   dagric-desktop-defaults  the owner's desktop and browser defaults.
//                            These change what the machine LOOKS LIKE and how
//                            it BEHAVES. dagric.list's own comment argues the
//                            owner should consent to those, and that argument
//                            still holds; it was simply being applied to the
//                            security baseline as well, by accident of there
//                            being one origin for all four.
//
// So: allow the origin, then blacklist the three. Blacklist entries are
// regular expressions matched against the package name, anchored here so
// "dagric-tools" cannot be matched by a future "dagric-toolsomething".
Unattended-Upgrade::Package-Blacklist {
    "^dagric-tools$";
    "^dagric-branding$";
    "^dagric-desktop-defaults$";
};
